2026-08-29 [HUMAN, verbatim, on doors]: "What I want is INSIDE EDGE of door corners detection, then geometry lines connecting all 4 corners. Not all image gen is going to make a perfect rectangle. But by extension of this logic, we may need to consider edge detection for non-consistent geometry as well. Let's just take a medieval door that's a standard wooden door with a half circle top for example." With an example frame (design/audit/door-corner-inside-edge-2026-08-29.jpg): the detected corner (blue) sat inside the dark void ~55 px left of the frame's actual inside corner (pink). RULING: an aperture is the INSIDE EDGE of its frame/reveal — the four (or more) corners where the jamb's inner edge meets head and sill/threshold — joined by lines into a polygon; the void's dark run is evidence for finding it, never the aperture itself. Non-rectangular heads (a half-round arch) are a contour: corners plus sampled points along the head, so the through-view clip, the leaf sprite fit and the warp pins follow the painted shape. Queued as the next door-detector item after delivery (B-DELIVERY).
